{"records":[{"id":"xn3vv2cdnk","title":"High-throughput CRISPR screens to dissect macrophage-Shigella interactions","description":"The positive hits, negative hits, and non-targeting controls of genome-wide CRISPR screens; Supplemental Tables.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-07-23T06:18:30.182Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xn3vv2cdnk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xn3vv2cdnk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yong","last_name":"LAI","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Genetic Screening","Host-Pathogen Interaction","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hfbhym45fz","title":"CRISPR screening reveals a requirement for ribosome recycling in efficient SARS-CoV-2 programmed ribosomal frameshifting and viral replication. Rehfeld et al.","description":"Supplemental Table S1. Results of SARS-CoV-2 programmed ribosomal frameshifting genome-wide CRISPR screens (MAGeCK analysis).\nSupplemental Table S2. Oligonucleotides.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-01-19T12:17:58.922Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hfbhym45fz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hfbhym45fz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Joshua","last_name":"Mendell","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Frederick","last_name":"Rehfeld","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/0003xa228","name":"Cancer Prevention and Research Institute of Texas","grant_id":"RP220309"},{"id":"https://ror.org/00np6vq88","name":"Welch Foundation","grant_id":"I-1961-20210327"},{"id":"https://ror.org/006w34k90","name":"Howard Hughes Medical Institute","grant_id":null}],"categories":["Oligonucleotide","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"rhsw3nbjhz","title":"Essential gene profiles for human pluripotent stem cells identify uncharacterized genes and substrate dependencies. Mair et al.","description":"Human pluripotent stem cells (hPSCs) provide an invaluable tool for modeling diseases and hold promise for regenerative medicine. For understanding pluripotency and lineage differentiation mechanisms, a critical first step involves systematically cataloging genes that are indispensable for hPSC maintenance and proliferation. To map genetic determinants of hPSC fitness, we performed genome-scale loss-of-function screens in an inducible Cas9 H1 hPSC line cultured on feeder cells and feeder-free to identify essential genes. Among these, we found FOXH1 and VENTX, genes that encode transcription factors previously implicated in stem cell biology, as well as an uncharacterized gene, C22orf43/DRICH1. hPSCs essential genes are substantially different from other cell lines, and gene essentiality in hPSCs is highly context-dependent with respect to different growth substrates. Our CRISPR screens establish parameters for genome-wide screens in hPSCs, which will facilitate the characterization of unappreciated regulators of hPSC biology to understand the genetic wiring of hPSC fitness and pluripotency.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-01-24T11:57:50.932Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rhsw3nbjhz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rhsw3nbjhz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Barbara","last_name":"Mair","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jelena","last_name":"Tomic","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sanna","last_name":"Masud","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jason","last_name":"Moffat","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Stem Cell","Functional Genomics","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"zdnrtmyfgh","title":"A Chemical-Genetic Interaction Matrix Reveals Drug Mechanism and Genetic Architecture","description":"This dataset is associated with an article bearing the same name ( https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.01.28.701823 ), describing the results of >400 CRISPR/Cas9 chemical-genetic genome-wide knockout screens covering 305 different compounds and 5 conditions. The dataset also contains meta-data about each compound as well as the raw and/or processed data necessary to reproduce various analyses and figures from the article. The contents of each table and sub-table (tab) are described in the overview tab of each file. Table contents are briefly described below.\nTable S1. Summary of the CGI dataset. (A) Sequenced samples. (B) Independent screens. (C) Compound details and verification. (D) NALM-6 gene expression. (E) CGIs detected.\nTable S2. CRANKS scores.\nTable S3. Known compound-target relationships.\nTable S4. Combined CRANKS scores for two independent TRAIL screens.\nTable S5. Jaccard similarity indices between screens in the CGI dataset. Pairs used in Fig. 2D are indicated by class.\nTable S6. Compound-transporter CGI scores.\nTable S7. (A) Compound dose-to-solubility ratios by screen. (B) Correlation coefficients of CRANKS scores with compound dose-to-solubility ratios. \nTable S8. Vemurafenib SSL hits and Gene Ontology annotations.\nTable S9. Predicted basic pKa and logP for compounds represented in the screen similarity network.\nTable S10. Mean genome-wide CRANKS score profile of the 28 screens in the phospholipidosis cluster.\nTable S11. LipidTOX Red assay determinations. (A) Cell counts and average LipidTOX levels. (B) Compound classification.\nTable S12. Average CRANKS scores for combined screens. (A) Nocodazole. (B) Active site mTOR inhibitors. (C) Rapamycin. (D) AICAR. (E) Pyridostatin and topoisomerase II inhibitors. (F) DHFR inhibitor screens. (G) PARP inhibitors. (H) CHEK1 inhibitors.\nTable S13. Comparison of CGIs for 9 shared compounds in this dataset and the Olivieri et al (2020) dataset. \nTable S14. Data for assessment of compound synergy/antagonism, including luminescence readings, treatments by well and log2 Bliss scores determined by compound pair.\nTable S15. (A) CRANKS2 scores. (B) Top 20,000 most correlated gene pairs.\nTable S16. RNA-seq data. (A) FK-506 versus DMSO and read counts by gene. (B) PPP3CA knockout versus wild-type controls. (C) PPP3R1 knockout versus wild-type controls. (D) Transcription rates in PABIR1 knockout clones.\nTable S17. List of DDR-associated genes.\nTable S18. (A) Significant exon-level CGIs. (B) Exon classification and validation data.\nTable S19. (A) Hypothetical gene CGIs with p-values <0.002 or absolute scores ≥2.5. (B) Hypothetical gene classification and mass spectral counts.\nTable S20. Correlation between gene CRANKS scores and population doublings.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-02-09T12:56:30.064Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/zdnrtmyfgh","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/zdnrtmyfgh"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jasmin","last_name":"Coulombe-Huntington","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01gavpb45","name":"Canadian Institutes of Health Research","grant_id":"FDN-167277, FDN–167265, PJT190250, PJT152920, PJT-451236, 163051, 201311MFE"},{"id":"https://ror.org/000az4664","name":"Canada Foundation for Innovation","grant_id":null},{"id":"https://ror.org/01h531d29","name":"Natural Sciences and Engineering Research Council of Canada","grant_id":"RGPIN-2022-04206 Canada Research Chairs, https://ror.org/0517h6h17"},{"id":"https://ror.org/05vd00053","name":"Terry Fox Foundation","grant_id":"https://ror.org/05vd00053, TFF-242122"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","grant_id":"R01 OD010929, R35 GM127032, RO1 NCI80728, RO1 NCI98571"},{"id":"https://ror.org/000dswa46","name":"W. M. Keck Foundation","grant_id":null},{"id":null,"name":"IVADO","grant_id":"PD-2018b-AP-05"},{"id":null,"name":"McLaughlin Centre Accelerator","grant_id":"MC-2023-07, MC-2024-08-3"},{"id":"https://ror.org/043q8yx54","name":"Ontario Institute for Cancer Research","grant_id":"https://ror.org/043q8yx54, SU2C-AACR-DT-19–15"},{"id":null,"name":"Institute for Research in Immunology and Cancer","grant_id":"https://ror.org/00wj6x496, SPC1901, SPC2102, SPC2104, La Fondation Famille Godin"},{"id":"https://ror.org/04m0xav37","name":"Leukemia and Lymphoma Society","grant_id":"MC_UU_00035/13"},{"id":"https://ror.org/029s29983","name":"Genome Canada","grant_id":"LSARP4524, LSARP13528, SU2C-AACR-DT-19–15, Centre for Advanced Proteomic and Chemogenomic Analyses"},{"id":"https://ror.org/00xhtr962","name":"Génome Québec","grant_id":"https://ror.org/00xhtr962, LSARP4524, Centre for Advanced Proteomic and Chemogenomic Analyses"},{"id":"https://ror.org/02fnpv153","name":"American Association For Cancer Research","grant_id":"https://ror.org/02fnpv153, SU2C-AACR-DT-19–15"}],"categories":["Genetics","Bioinformatics","Systems Biology","Pharmacogenomics","Gene Knockout","Computational Biology","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"mnpg6dp8fh","title":"Genotoxic effects of base and prime editing in human hematopoietic stem cells","description":"Raw data associated to the manuscript by Fiumara et al. Base and prime editors (BEs and PEs) may provide more precise genetic engineering than nuclease-based approaches because they bypass the dependence on DNA double-strand breaks. However, little is known about their cellular responses and genotoxicity. Here, we compared state-of-the-art BEs and PEs and Cas9 in human hematopoietic stem and progenitor cells with respect to editing efficiency, cytotoxicity, transcriptomic changes and on-target and genome-wide genotoxicity. BEs and PEs induced detrimental transcriptional responses that reduced editing efficiency and hematopoietic repopulation in xenotransplants and also generated DNA double-strand breaks and genotoxic byproducts, including deletions and translocations, at a lower frequency than Cas9. These effects were strongest for cytidine BEs due to suboptimal inhibition of base excision repair and were mitigated by tailoring delivery timing and editor expression through optimized mRNA design. However, BEs altered the mutational landscape of hematopoietic stem and progenitor cells across the genome by increasing the load and relative proportions of nucleotide variants. These findings raise concerns about the genotoxicity of BEs and PEs and warrant further investigation in view of their clinical application.","source":{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-08-21T09:44:58.573Z","url":"https://ordr.hsr.it/datasets/mnpg6dp8fh","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/mnpg6dp8fh"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Martina","last_name":"Fiumara","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Samuele","last_name":"Ferrari","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Luigi","last_name":"Naldini","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biotechnology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"3k2rtfv9bx","title":"CRISPR gene scores (K562 cells that lost KIR3DS1-Fc staining)","description":"This file indicates CRISPR gene scores for 18,350 genes targeted by sgRNAs in K562 cells that were selected for loss of KIR3DS1-Fc binding.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-11-08T12:58:06.31Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/3k2rtfv9bx","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/3k2rtfv9bx"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Wilfredo","last_name":"Garcia Beltran","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"k6wm46g4tw","title":"Data set for the paper entitled \"CRISPR-enabled genetic screens identify synthetic lethal targets across frequently altered cancer drivers\" by Desjardins, Bowlan, et al. (2026)","description":"We undertook a series of genome-wide CRISPR screens using functionally validated isogenic cell lines and mapped SL interactions across 15 genetic alterations characteristic of diseases with high incidence and unmet clinical need: FBXW7, CCNE1, CDK12, ARID1A, KMT2D, DNMT3A, TET2, KEAP1, STK11, IDH1, SF3B1, SRSF2, U2AF1, chromosome 18q loss, and chromosome 13q loss. The data deposited here make the raw read count files available for all CRISPR screens conducted in the study. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-01-26T11:43:11.208Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/k6wm46g4tw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/k6wm46g4tw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jordan","last_name":"Young","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alejandro","last_name":"Álvarez-Quilón","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jessica","last_name":"Desjardins","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Julian","last_name":"Bowlan","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Primary Data"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"2w649fccnt","title":"High-throughput mapping of long-range neuronal projection using in situ sequencing, Chen et al., dataset 4","description":"Raw in situ sequencing data and other images for the paper of the same name. Part of four related datasets:\n10.17632/mk82s9x82t.1\n10.17632/g7kdxznt6w.1\n10.17632/86wf7xfz5x.1\n10.17632/2w649fccnt.1\n\nThese datasets demonstrate BARseq, a highly multiplexed projection mapping method based on in situ sequencing of RNA barcodes. These data further demonstrates the use of BARseq to correlate gene expression and projections at cellular resolution with high throughput, and the use of BARseq to map projections of Cre-labeled subpopulation of neurons. \nThese datasets Also contain two single-cell RNAseq datasets. One single-cell RNAseq dataset identified four Intratelencephalic neuron subtypes in mouse auditory cortex. A second single-cell RNAseq dataset compared endogenous gene expression in barcoded vs. non-barcoded neurons and found that information about endogenous gene expression can be successfully recovered from the barcoded neurons.\n\n\nDescription of files in the current dataset:\n\nDataset 4: \nRNAseq analysis.zip: contains two datasets for reproducing the analysis of (1) transcriptomic clustering of non-barcoded neurons in the auditory cortex, and (2) comparison of barcoded vs. non-barcoded cells using single-cell RNAseq.\nadditionalhelpers.zip: additional MATLAB helper functions that may be required to process BARseq datasets.\nXC91-234 images and processing script2.zip: part of the XC91 dataset. Unzip to the same folder to run scripts.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-10-04T05:05:09.864Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/2w649fccnt","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/2w649fccnt"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Xiaoyin","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Neuroscience"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"75brhkh25r","title":"A genome-wide hepatocyte CRISPR-CAS9 screen to identify host factors essential for optimal Plasmodium liver stage development. ","description":"To identify host genes critical for Plasmodium LS development, we used the GeCKOv2 sgRNA pool to generate a whole-genome knockout library in HepG2-CD81 cells (Sanjana et al., 2014; Shalem et al., 2014). HepG2-CD81 cells were transduced with lentivirus containing the pooled GeCKOv2 sgRNA library of 114,123 sgRNAs targeting 19,031 protein-coding genes (~6 sgRNAs/gene) and selected in puromycin for 5-7 days. To evaluate sgRNA diversity in the HepG2-CD81-GeCKOv2 library, we PCR-amplified the integrated sgRNA cassettes from genomic DNA extracted from transduced cells and subjected the amplified library to Illumina sequencing. At the gene level, sgRNAs targeting all but 15 of 19,031 (99.92%) protein-coding genes were observed. Twelve to fourteen days after post-transduction, we infected forty million puromycin-resistant cells with a green fluorescent protein (GFP) expressing-Plasmodium yoelii at a multiplicity of infection (MOI) of 0.3. After 24 h of infection, cells were sorted into infected and bystander cell populations by GFP signal intensity with fluorescence-activated cell sorting (FACS). Separately, a parallel culture of uninfected cells was also maintained to normalize the sgRNA frequency distributions. We obtained four independent biological replicates with library generation and sequencing occurring in parallel. Genes with significantly enriched sgRNAs were identified for both the bystander and infected populations when compared to uninfected cells. Cells that harbor genetic alterations restricting P. yoelii development (i.e., sgRNAs that target host genes important for infection) were expected to be enriched in the uninfected bin; we termed this group ‘putative positive regulators of infection’. We categorized sgRNAs enriched in the infected cells as ‘putative negative regulators of infection’. In this initial screen, we identified 242 sgRNAs that were statistically enriched in infected or bystander groups after accounting for multiple hypotheses. There were 67 sgRNAs significantly enriched in the infected cells compared to uninfected cells and 175 genes were significantly enriched in the bystander bin relative to the uninfected bin. \n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-08-27T07:31:54.012Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/75brhkh25r","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/75brhkh25r"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kamalakannan","last_name":"Vijayan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nadia","last_name":"Arang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ling ","last_name":"Wei","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Robert","last_name":"Morrison","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rechel ","last_name":"Geiger","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rachael ","last_name":"Parks","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Adam J ","last_name":"Lewis","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Fred D ","last_name":"Mast ","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alyse N ","last_name":"Douglass","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Heather S ","last_name":"Kain","orcid":null},{"first_name":"John D","last_name":"Aitchison ","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jarrod S ","last_name":"Johnson","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alan ","last_name":"Aderem","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alexis","last_name":"Kaushansky","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"nxvdzybwhf","title":"Illuminating host-mycobacterial interactions with genome-wide CRISPR knockout and CRISPRi screens","description":"The data deposited here contains genome-wide CRISPR knockout and CRISPRi screen data, including the positive hits, the negative hits, and non-targeting controls. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-08-30T11:32:01.108Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nxvdzybwhf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nxvdzybwhf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yong","last_name":"Lai","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Functional Genomics","High-Throughput Sequencing","Tuberculosis"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":125753,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":154,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":125753,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":125264,"restricted":61,"embargoed":428},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2138},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1335},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":614},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":451},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":426},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":392},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":390},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":356},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":314},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":262}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":123164},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1894},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":181},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":128},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":73},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":60},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":53},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":39},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":30},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":869},{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":785},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":481},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":402},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":358}]}}